## Completed work ##

epoll_system
	client_fd EPOLLIN -> client_request ready
	backend_fd EPOLLOUT -> backend ready to listen for the request
	backend_fd EPOLLIN -> response ready from the server
	client_fd EPOLLOUT -> client ready to get the response

state machine:
	STATE_LISTENER: subscribing new client to epoll_system with its own client_socket
	STATE_READ_CLIENT: ready to read data from client
	STATE_INSPECT_REQUEST: ready to inspect request
	STATE_SEND_BACKEND: sending req to backend
	STATE_READ_BACKEND: reading response from backend
	STATE_SEND_CLIENT: sending resp to client_fd

URI Normalization:
	-General decoding & transformation:
		- ASCII Decoding: %XX to ascii
		- %u Decoding: Decoding Unicode encodings
		- Bad Unescape Detection: malformed encodings %RR
	- Path & Directory Handling:
		- Multiple Slashes Norm: //// => /
		- Directory Traversal Normalization: ../ => ./
		- IIS Backslashes: \ => /

AHO_CORASICK algorithm:
    - Used for fast exact-match detection in the WAF.
    - All fixed rule patterns are inserted into one trie during rule loading.
    - Each final trie node stores the rule linked to the matched pattern.
    - Failure links are then built so the engine can continue matching without
      restarting from zero when a character does not match.
    Runtime:
        - After HTTP parsing and normalization, the WAF scans the URI, headers,
          and body through the automaton.
        - The input is scanned only once.
        - If the current node contains a rule, the engine checks if the rule zone
          matches the inspected part: URI, HEADER, BODY, or ANY.
        - If a match is valid, the request is marked malicious and can be blocked.
    Why it is efficient:
        - It checks all exact-match rules at the same time.
        - Complexity is linear: O(input size + matches).
        - No regex cost, no per-rule scanning, and no per-request trie allocation.
    Limitation:
        - It only detects fixed strings.
        - Regex/PCRE2 is needed later for flexible evasions like variable spaces,
          encodings, optional characters, or complex attack patterns.
	
